IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `scr-dates`
IMPORT `scr-duties`

-- Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025 (Vic) -- the PARTY's questions about costs and money: Order 62
-- (security for costs), Order 63 Parts 1 to 3 (who pays costs, and on what basis) with Appendix A Sections 1 and 4 (the
-- most allowed per hour for lawyers and per day for counsel), Order 79 (money paid into court, especially for a person
-- under disability) and Order 85 (procedure under the Legal Identity of Defendants (Organisational Child Abuse) Act 2018).
--
-- SEAT: the individual who is a party to a civil proceeding in the Supreme Court of Victoria, usually the person bringing
-- the claim (an injured person, a child abuse survivor suing an unincorporated organisation, a person under disability
-- through a litigation guardian): "Can I be made to give security for costs?", "Who pays the costs of this step?", "How
-- much of my lawyer's hourly rate can I get back?", "Can the money paid into court for me be paid out?", "By when must I
-- name the proper defendant?". The Court's, the Senior Master's and the Costs Court's orders are asked as facts.
--
-- ANSWERS as in scr-process.l4. The Court is taken not to have ordered the Christmas vacation counted (r 3.04(1)).
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Supreme Court
-- (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025, S.R. No. 85/2025, Version No. 003 (1 January 2026), not the authorised version.
-- (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be
-- reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026
-- (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Supreme Court civil procedure: security, costs, money in court, and child abuse claims against organisations`

§§ `Order 62 -- security for costs`

-- r 62.02(1): "Where -- (a) the plaintiff is ordinarily resident out of Victoria; (b) the plaintiff is a corporation or (not
-- being a plaintiff who sues in a representative capacity) sues, not for the plaintiff's own benefit, but for the benefit of
-- some other person, and there is reason to believe that the plaintiff has insufficient assets in Victoria to pay the costs
-- of the defendant if ordered to do so; (c) a proceeding by the plaintiff in another court for the same claim is pending;
-- (d) subject to paragraph (2), the address of the plaintiff is not stated or is not stated correctly in the plaintiff's
-- originating process; (e) the plaintiff has changed the plaintiff's address after the commencement of the proceeding in
-- order to avoid the consequences of the proceeding; (f) under any Act the Court may require security for costs -- the
-- Court, on the application of a defendant, may order that the plaintiff give security for the costs of the defendant ...
-- and that the proceeding as against that defendant be stayed until the security is given." (2): "The Court shall not
-- require a plaintiff to give security by reason only of paragraph (1)(d) if in failing to state the plaintiff's address or
-- to state the plaintiff's correct address the plaintiff acted innocently and without intention to deceive."
-- None of (a) to (f): NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (the Court's inherent power is not encoded).
@export As a plaintiff, can I be ordered to give security for the defendant's costs, with my case stayed until I do (r 62.02)? The Court may order it if I live outside Victoria, sue for someone else's benefit with too few assets in Victoria, have another case on the same claim pending, gave no or a wrong address in my writ (unless innocently), moved to dodge the case, or an Act allows it.
GIVEN `do I ordinarily live outside Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are ordinarily resident out of Victoria
      `do I sue for someone else's benefit with too few assets here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you sue not for your own benefit but for someone else's (other than in a representative capacity) and there is reason to believe you lack assets in Victoria to pay the defendant's costs
      `is another case of mine on the same claim pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proceeding by you in another court for the same claim is pending
      `is my address missing or wrong on my writ` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your address is not stated, or not stated correctly, in your originating process
      `was that innocent, with no intention to deceive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether you acted innocently and without intention to deceive
      `did I change address to avoid the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you changed your address after the case began in order to avoid its consequences
      `does an Act let the Court require security` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Act lets the Court require security for costs in your case
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to give security for costs` MEANS
    IF    `do I ordinarily live outside Victoria`
       OR `do I sue for someone else's benefit with too few assets here`
       OR `is another case of mine on the same claim pending`
       OR `did I change address to avoid the case`
       OR `does an Act let the Court require security`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `is my address missing or wrong on my writ`
         THEN IF `was that innocent, with no intention to deceive`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

-- r 62.04: "Where a plaintiff fails to give the security required by an order, the Court may dismiss the plaintiff's claim."
-- No order for security: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*. The security given: JUST FALSE ("where" fails).
@export If I have been ordered to give security for costs and do not, can my claim be dismissed (r 62.04)? Yes, the Court may dismiss it. The Court may also vary or set aside the order (r 62.05).
GIVEN `have I been ordered to give security` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered you to give security for costs
      `have I given it as ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the security in the manner and by the time the Court directed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my claim be dismissed for want of security` MEANS
    IF (NOT `have I been ordered to give security`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `have I given it as ordered`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `Order 63 Parts 1 to 3 -- who pays costs, and on what basis`

-- r 63.13: "Subject to these Rules, a party to a proceeding shall not be entitled to recover any costs of the proceeding
-- from any other party except by order of the Court." Rules that make a party liable without an order include rr 25.08(3),
-- 63.14, 63.15, 63.16.1 and 63.18 (r 63.10(d): such costs may be taxed without an order).
@export Can I recover my costs from another party without a court order (r 63.13)? No, unless a rule itself makes that party liable (for example on a discontinuance, r 63.15, or a dismissal for inactivity, r 25.08(3)).
GIVEN `has the Court ordered the costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered the other party to pay your costs
      `does a rule make them liable without an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a rule itself makes the other party liable for your costs (for example they discontinued, their case was dismissed for inactivity, they sought an extension of time, or they disputed a fact later proved)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover my costs` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered the costs` OR `does a rule make them liable without an order`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 63.14: "Where a party applies for an extension or abridgement of any time fixed by these Rules or by any order fixing,
-- extending or abridging time, that party, unless the Court otherwise orders, shall pay the costs of and occasioned by the
-- application."
@export If I apply to extend or shorten a time fixed by the Rules or an order, must I pay the costs of the application (r 63.14)? Yes, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made a different order about the costs of your application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay the costs of my extension application` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `Step whose costs I ask about` IS ONE OF
    `an amendment of a pleading`
    `an interlocutory application with no order, or an order silent on costs`
    `an application or step whose costs were reserved`

-- r 63.17: amendment: "the costs of and occasioned by the amendment and the costs of any application for leave to make the
-- amendment are the parties' costs in the proceeding, unless the Court otherwise orders." r 63.20: an interlocutory
-- application with "(a) no order ... made on the application; or (b) the order made is silent as to costs -- the costs are
-- the parties' costs in the proceeding, unless the Court otherwise orders." r 63.22: reserved costs "are the parties' costs
-- in the proceeding, unless the Court otherwise orders." ("Costs in the proceeding": they follow the costs order made at
-- the end of the case.)
@export Are the costs of this step "costs in the proceeding", following whatever costs order is made at the end of my case (rr 63.17, 63.20, 63.22)? Yes for an amendment of a pleading, an interlocutory application with no order or an order silent on costs, and reserved costs, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `which step` IS A `Step whose costs I ask about` @desc The step: an amendment of a pleading, an interlocutory application on which no order was made or the order says nothing about costs, or an application or step whose costs were reserved
      `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made a particular order about the costs of that step
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Are these costs in the proceeding` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 63.18: "Where a party serves a notice -- (a) under Rule 35.03(2) disputing a fact, and afterwards that fact is proved in
-- the proceeding; (b) under Rule 35.05(2) disputing the authenticity of a document, and afterwards the authenticity of that
-- document is proved in the proceeding -- that party shall pay the costs of proof, unless the Court otherwise orders."
-- Not disputed by notice, or not proved: JUST FALSE (the "where" fails).
@export If I served a notice disputing a fact (or a document's authenticity) and it was later proved, must I pay the costs of proving it (r 63.18)? Yes, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `did I serve a notice disputing it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you served a notice under rule 35.03(2) disputing the fact, or under rule 35.05(2) disputing the document's authenticity
      `was it afterwards proved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fact, or the document's authenticity, was afterwards proved in the proceeding
      `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered otherwise
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay the costs of proof` MEANS
    IF `did I serve a notice disputing it` AND `was it afterwards proved` AND (NOT `has the Court ordered otherwise`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `My side for costs` IS ONE OF
    `the side making the claim`
    `the side the claim is against`

DECLARE `What unpaid interlocutory costs can bring` IS ONE OF
    `my proceeding may be stayed or dismissed`
    `my defence may be struck out`

-- r 63.03(2): "Costs which a party is required to pay under any of these Rules or an order of the Court shall, unless the
-- Court otherwise orders, be paid forthwith." (3): "Where the Court makes an interlocutory order for costs, the Court may
-- then or thereafter order that if the party liable to pay the costs fails to do so -- (a) if that party is the plaintiff,
-- the proceeding shall be stayed or dismissed; (b) if that party is a defendant, the defendant's defence shall be struck
-- out." (4): plaintiff includes any person making a claim; defendant, any person against whom one is made.
-- No such order, or the costs paid: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export If I do not pay interlocutory costs ordered against me (costs are payable straight away unless the Court orders otherwise), what can happen (r 63.03)? If the Court has so ordered, a plaintiff's proceeding is stayed or dismissed, and a defendant's defence struck out.
GIVEN `my side` IS A `My side for costs` @desc Whether you are the side making the claim (plaintiff) or the side the claim is against (defendant)
      `has the Court made that order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that, if you fail to pay the interlocutory costs, your proceeding be stayed or dismissed or your defence struck out
      `have I failed to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have failed to pay the costs
GIVETH A MAYBE `What unpaid interlocutory costs can bring`
`What can follow unpaid interlocutory costs` MEANS
    IF `has the Court made that order` AND `have I failed to pay`
    THEN CONSIDER `my side`
         WHEN `the side making the claim` THEN JUST `my proceeding may be stayed or dismissed`
         WHEN `the side the claim is against` THEN JUST `my defence may be struck out`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- r 63.20.1: "If an order for costs is made on an interlocutory application or hearing, the party in whose favour the order
-- is made shall not tax those costs until the proceeding in which the order is made is completed, unless the Court orders
-- that the costs may be taxed immediately."
@export I won an order for the costs of an interlocutory application: may I have them taxed (assessed) now (r 63.20.1)? Not until the proceeding is completed, unless the Court orders that they may be taxed immediately.
GIVEN `is the proceeding completed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding in which the order was made is completed
      `has the Court ordered immediate taxation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court ordered that the costs may be taxed immediately
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I tax my interlocutory costs now` MEANS
    IF `is the proceeding completed` OR `has the Court ordered immediate taxation`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `The basis of taxation` IS ONE OF
    `the standard basis: all costs reasonably incurred and of reasonable amount`
    `the indemnity basis: all costs except those of unreasonable amount or unreasonably incurred, any doubt resolved in my favour`
    `the basis the Court directed`

-- r 63.28: costs are taxed on "(a) the standard basis; (b) the indemnity basis; or (c) such other basis as the Court may
-- direct." r 63.31: "Except as provided by these Rules or any order of the Court, including the Costs Court, costs shall be
-- taxed on the standard basis." r 63.30: "On a taxation on the standard basis, all costs reasonably incurred and of
-- reasonable amount shall be allowed." r 63.30.1: "(1) ... on the indemnity basis all costs shall be allowed except in so
-- far as they are of an unreasonable amount or have been unreasonably incurred. (2) Any doubt ... shall be resolved in
-- favour of the party to whom the costs are payable." (r 26.08 gives the indemnity basis after some offers.)
@export On what basis will the costs payable to me be taxed (rr 63.28 to 63.31)? The standard basis (costs reasonably incurred and of reasonable amount), unless a rule (such as rule 26.08 after an offer) or the Court gives the indemnity basis (all costs except unreasonable ones, doubts resolved in my favour) or another basis.
GIVEN `does a rule or order give the indemnity basis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a rule (for example rule 26.08 after an unaccepted offer) or a Court order gives you costs on the indemnity basis
      `has the Court directed another basis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has directed some other basis
GIVETH A `The basis of taxation`
`On what basis are my costs taxed` MEANS
    IF `has the Court directed another basis`
    THEN `the basis the Court directed`
    ELSE IF `does a rule or order give the indemnity basis`
         THEN `the indemnity basis: all costs except those of unreasonable amount or unreasonably incurred, any doubt resolved in my favour`
         ELSE `the standard basis: all costs reasonably incurred and of reasonable amount`

-- r 63.23(1): "Where a solicitor for a party ... has caused costs to be incurred improperly or without reasonable cause or to
-- be wasted by a failure to act with reasonable competence and expedition, the Court may make an order that -- (a) all or
-- any of the costs between the solicitor and the client be disallowed or that the solicitor repay to the client ...; (b) the
-- solicitor pay to the solicitor's client all or any of the costs which the client has been ordered to pay to any party;
-- (c) the solicitor pay all or any of the costs payable by any party other than the client." (3): "The Court shall not make
-- an order under paragraph (1) without giving the solicitor a reasonable opportunity to be heard." (7): barristers too.
-- No such costs caused: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*. Not yet heard: JUST FALSE ("shall not ... without").
@export Can my own lawyer be ordered to bear or repay costs they caused improperly, without reasonable cause, or by failing to act with reasonable competence and speed (r 63.23)? Yes, the Court may disallow their costs, make them repay me, or make them pay the costs I was ordered to pay, but only after giving them a reasonable opportunity to be heard. This applies to barristers too.
GIVEN `did my lawyer cause costs improperly or waste them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your solicitor or barrister caused costs to be incurred improperly or without reasonable cause, or wasted them by failing to act with reasonable competence and expedition (for example failing to attend, file a document or be ready)
      `has the lawyer had a reasonable opportunity to be heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the lawyer has been given a reasonable opportunity to be heard by the Court on it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my lawyer be made to pay the costs` MEANS
    IF (NOT `did my lawyer cause costs improperly or waste them`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `has the lawyer had a reasonable opportunity to be heard`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Appendix A -- the scale: most allowed per hour and per day (r 63.34)`

DECLARE `Who did the work` IS ONE OF
    `a legal practitioner, doing legal work`
    `an employee who is not a legal practitioner, doing legal work`
    `an employee who is not a legal practitioner, doing clerical work`
    `a supporting professional (accountant, financial adviser, IT specialist, analyst)`

-- r 63.34(1): costs are "taxed in accordance with Appendix A, unless the Court, or ... the Costs Court, otherwise orders."
-- (2): whatever the basis. (3): "on special grounds arising out of the nature and importance or the difficulty or urgency
-- of the case, may allow an increase, not exceeding 30 per cent, of the maximums set out in Sections 1 and 4 of Appendix A".
-- Appendix A ("in respect of work done on and after 1 January 2026"), Section 1: "1.1 (a) ... a legal practitioner of 10 or
-- more years post-admission experience ... up to a maximum of $918 per hour; (b) ... between 5 and 9 years ... $663 per hour;
-- (c) ... less than 5 years ... $459 per hour. 1.2 ... an employee of a law practice ... who is not a legal practitioner --
-- (a) doing work requiring legal skill or knowledge ... $398 per hour; (b) doing work not requiring legal skill or knowledge,
-- capable of performance by a clerk ... $296 per hour; (c) exercising professional skills and expertise complementing or
-- supporting the delivery of legal services ... $677 per hour." The scale is exclusive of GST.
-- Experience is counted in completed years (fork F16: the bands "between 5 and 9" and "10 or more" leave a part year after
-- 9 unplaced; finding 6). Work before 1 January 2026, or an increase over 30%: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text* (the
-- earlier scale is not deposited) / *limited by its own wording*.
GIVEN `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` IS A NUMBER, `the maximum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`r 63.34(3) -- the maximum with the increase` `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` `the maximum` MEANS
    IF `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` GREATER THAN 30 OR `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` LESS THAN 0
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the maximum` TIMES (100 PLUS `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent`) DIVIDED BY 100)

@export What is the most per hour that can be allowed for this person's work when the costs payable to me are taxed (r 63.34, Appendix A Section 1)? For work from 1 January 2026: a lawyer of 10 or more years $918, of 5 to 9 years $663, under 5 years $459; a non-lawyer employee doing legal work $398, clerical work $296; a supporting professional $677 (all excluding GST); the Court may raise these by up to 30% on special grounds.
GIVEN `who did the work` IS A `Who did the work` @desc Who did the work: a legal practitioner doing legal work, a non-lawyer employee of the law practice doing legal work or clerical work, or a supporting professional (accountant, financial adviser, IT specialist, data or forensic analyst)
      `the lawyer's years since admission` IS A NUMBER @desc If a legal practitioner: their completed years of post-admission experience (0 otherwise)
      `was the work done on or after 1 January 2026` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work was done on or after 1 January 2026 (the scale applies to that work)
      `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` IS A NUMBER @desc Any increase the Court (or the Costs Court) allowed on special grounds, in per cent, at most 30 (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most per hour for this work` MEANS
    IF (NOT `was the work done on or after 1 January 2026`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE `r 63.34(3) -- the maximum with the increase` `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` `the maximum`
    WHERE
        `the completed years` MEANS FLOOR `the lawyer's years since admission`
        `the maximum` MEANS
            CONSIDER `who did the work`
            WHEN `a legal practitioner, doing legal work` THEN
                IF `the completed years` AT LEAST 10
                THEN 918
                ELSE IF `the completed years` AT LEAST 5
                     THEN 663
                     ELSE 459
            WHEN `an employee who is not a legal practitioner, doing legal work` THEN 398
            WHEN `an employee who is not a legal practitioner, doing clerical work` THEN 296
            WHEN `a supporting professional (accountant, financial adviser, IT specialist, analyst)` THEN 677

DECLARE `Counsel` IS ONE OF
    `junior counsel`
    `senior counsel`

DECLARE `Counsel's work` IS ONE OF
    `an appearance day (with that day's conferences, preparation and advice)`
    `an hour of conferences, preparation or advice on another day`

-- Appendix A Section 4.1: "the fees to be allowed in relation to counsel's fees are reasonable amounts up to the following
-- maximums -- (a) For appearances (including any conferences, preparation, advice or other work on the day of hearing), per
-- day: Junior Counsel $7442, Senior Counsel $11 163; (b) For conferences, preparation, advice or other work on other days,
-- per hour: $744, $1116." 4.2: circuit fees are additional.
@export What is the most that can be allowed for a barrister's fee when the costs payable to me are taxed (r 63.34, Appendix A Section 4)? For work from 1 January 2026: per appearance day, junior counsel $7442 and senior counsel $11,163; per hour on other days, $744 and $1116 (excluding GST); the Court may raise these by up to 30% on special grounds.
GIVEN `which counsel` IS A `Counsel` @desc Whether the barrister is junior counsel or senior counsel (SC or KC)
      `which work` IS A `Counsel's work` @desc An appearance day (including that day's conferences, preparation and advice), or an hour of work on another day
      `was the work done on or after 1 January 2026` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work was done on or after 1 January 2026
      `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` IS A NUMBER @desc Any increase the Court allowed on special grounds, in per cent, at most 30 (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most for counsel's fee` MEANS
    IF (NOT `was the work done on or after 1 January 2026`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE `r 63.34(3) -- the maximum with the increase` `the increase the Court allowed, in per cent` `the counsel maximum`
    WHERE
        `the counsel maximum` MEANS
            CONSIDER `which counsel`
            WHEN `junior counsel` THEN
                CONSIDER `which work`
                WHEN `an appearance day (with that day's conferences, preparation and advice)` THEN 7442
                WHEN `an hour of conferences, preparation or advice on another day` THEN 744
            WHEN `senior counsel` THEN
                CONSIDER `which work`
                WHEN `an appearance day (with that day's conferences, preparation and advice)` THEN 11163
                WHEN `an hour of conferences, preparation or advice on another day` THEN 1116

§§ `Order 79 -- money paid into court`

-- r 79.02(2): "Subject to paragraph (3), money paid into court and any interest allocated or received in respect of that
-- money shall not be paid out except by order of the Court." (3): "Except where money is paid into court for the benefit of
-- a person under disability, money paid into court ... may be paid out by the Senior Master on and in accordance with a
-- written authority signed by each party to the proceeding or the party's solicitor, each signature of a party to be
-- verified by the indorsed certificate of a solicitor."
@export Can money paid into the Supreme Court in my case be paid out without a court order (r 79.02)? Never where it was paid in for a person under disability (a minor or person unable to manage their affairs); otherwise yes, by the Senior Master on a written authority signed by every party (or their solicitor), with each party's signature certified by a solicitor.
GIVEN `was it paid in for a person under disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the money was paid into court for the benefit of a person under disability (for example a child's or injured person's compensation)
      `is there a written authority signed by every party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a written authority for payment out signed by each party to the proceeding (or its solicitor)
      `is each party's signature certified by a solicitor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each party's signature is verified by a solicitor's certificate indorsed on the authority
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the money be paid out without a court order` MEANS
    IF `was it paid in for a person under disability`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `is there a written authority signed by every party` AND `is each party's signature certified by a solicitor`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 79.04(3): "The party ordered to pay into court shall -- (a) pay the money to the Senior Master; and (b) within 7 days of
-- the payment serve on the party who obtained the order notice in writing of the payment."
@export If money was ordered to be paid into court for my benefit as a person under disability, by what day must the paying party give my side written notice of the payment (r 79.04(3)(b))? Within 7 days of the payment.
GIVEN `the day of the payment` IS A DATE @desc The day the money was paid to the Senior Master, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I be told of the payment into court` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 7 `days after` `the day of the payment` FALSE) FALSE

-- r 79.06(1): "Where an order is made that a party pay money into court for the benefit of a person under disability, and it
-- appears to the Senior Master after due inquiry that loss has been occasioned to the person under disability -- (a) through
-- undue delay by the party or the party's solicitor in making the payment; or (b) where the order was made on the approval
-- of a compromise ..., through undue delay by the solicitor for or the litigation guardian of the person under disability
-- in -- (i) obtaining the approval of the compromise; (ii) procuring the authentication of the order; or (iii) serving a
-- copy of the order on the other party -- the Senior Master may order that the person responsible for the loss pay into
-- court for the benefit of the person under disability a sum by way of interest on the money received or to be received for
-- investment." (2): "The sum ... shall not exceed that derived by applying to the money for the period of the loss the last
-- rate fixed under the Penalty Interest Rates Act 1983." (3): the person responsible must be heard first.
-- The rate is not deposited (asked); simple interest over a 365-day year (fork F17).
-- No undue delay causing loss: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export If undue delay in paying money into court for me (as a person under disability), or in getting my settlement approved or the order authenticated or served, cost me interest, what is the most the Senior Master can order the person responsible to pay in for me (r 79.06)? Interest on the money for the period of the loss at the last penalty interest rate.
GIVEN `was there undue delay that caused me loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether undue delay by the paying party or its solicitor in paying in, or by your solicitor or litigation guardian in getting the compromise approved, the order authenticated or served, caused you loss
      `the amount of money` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of money received, or to be received, for investment, in dollars
      `the days of the loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days in the period of the loss
      `the last penalty interest rate, in per cent a year` IS A NUMBER @desc The last rate fixed under the Penalty Interest Rates Act 1983, in per cent a year
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most interest for the delay` MEANS
    IF `was there undue delay that caused me loss`
    THEN JUST (`the amount of money` TIMES `the last penalty interest rate, in per cent a year` DIVIDED BY 100 TIMES `the days of the loss` DIVIDED BY 365)
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `The judicial registrar's order on payment out` IS ONE OF
    `the application was dismissed, wholly or in part`
    `the application was granted, wholly or in part`

-- r 79.16(1): "Any person aggrieved by an order of the judicial registrar dismissing, or dismissing in part, an application
-- for a payment under Rule 79.02(2) may appeal that dismissal to the Court constituted by the Senior Master within one month
-- of the notification of the order to the applicant." (2): "Within 5 days of the making of an order by the judicial
-- registrar granting, or granting in part, an application ..., any person aggrieved by the order may appeal that grant ...
-- on the ground that the payment would not be in the best interests of the person for whom the Senior Master holds money".
-- Five days is "5 days or less": weekends are not counted (r 3.01(4)).
@export By what day may I appeal to the Senior Master against the judicial registrar's order on an application for payment out of money held in court for me (r 79.16)? A dismissal: within one month of the order being notified to the applicant. A grant: within 5 days of the order (weekends not counted), on the ground that the payment is not in the best interests of the person the money is held for.
GIVEN `what the order did` IS A `The judicial registrar's order on payment out` @desc Whether the judicial registrar dismissed (wholly or partly) or granted (wholly or partly) the application for payment out
      `the day the order was notified or made` IS A DATE @desc For a dismissal: the day the order was notified to the applicant; for a grant: the day the order was made (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when may I appeal the payment out order` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what the order did`
    WHEN `the application was dismissed, wholly or in part` THEN
        `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 1 `months after` `the day the order was notified or made` FALSE) FALSE
    WHEN `the application was granted, wholly or in part` THEN
        `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 5 `days after` `the day the order was notified or made` FALSE) FALSE

-- r 79.10(1): "This Rule applies where -- (a) money is held for the benefit of a person in respect of a claim by or on behalf
-- of that person in another court, whether that court is within or out of Victoria; and (b) if the claim were made in a
-- proceeding in the Court, that person would be a person under disability." (2): "Where -- (a) the money is held in
-- Victoria; or (b) the person for whose benefit the money is held is or is about to become domiciled or ordinarily resident
-- within Victoria -- the Senior Master may order that if the money is paid to the Court it be held in court for the benefit
-- of that person." (3): it is then treated as paid in for a person under disability.
-- The conditions not met: JUST FALSE ("where"; ruling (b)).
@export Money is held for me (as a minor or person unable to manage my affairs) from a claim in another court, in or outside Victoria: can the Supreme Court's Senior Master order it held in the Supreme Court for me (r 79.10)? Yes, if the money is held in Victoria, or I live in (or am about to move to) Victoria.
GIVEN `is money held for me from a claim in another court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether money is held for your benefit in respect of a claim by or for you in another court (in or outside Victoria)
      `would I be a person under disability in this Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would be a person under disability (a minor, or unable to manage your affairs) if the claim were in the Supreme Court
      `is the money held in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the money is held in Victoria
      `do I live or am I about to live in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are, or are about to become, domiciled or ordinarily resident in Victoria
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can money held for me elsewhere be held in this Court` MEANS
    IF `is money held for me from a claim in another court` AND `would I be a person under disability in this Court` AND (`is the money held in Victoria` OR `do I live or am I about to live in Victoria`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 85 -- child abuse claims against an unincorporated organisation (with Legal Identity of Defendants (Organisational Child Abuse) Act 2018 ss 7, 13 restated)`

-- Restated narrowly from the Legal Identity of Defendants (Organisational Child Abuse) Act 2018 (a module here cannot
-- import it): s 13: "Pending the nomination of an entity as a proper defendant under section 7 or the appointment of a
-- proper defendant in accordance with section 8 -- (a) a proceeding for a claim founded on or arising from child abuse may be
-- commenced or continue against an NGO in the name of that NGO; and (b) a proceeding for a claim may be commenced against an
-- NGO in its own name despite it being an unincorporated association or body." s 5 (NGO, an unincorporated association or
-- body) is asked as a fact. r 85.04: "An application under section 8 ... shall be made by summons."
-- A proper defendant already nominated or appointed: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (s 7(2): the nominee is then
-- the defendant for all purposes).
@export Can I start or continue my child abuse claim against an unincorporated organisation (NGO) in the organisation's own name while no proper defendant has been nominated or appointed (Legal Identity of Defendants (Organisational Child Abuse) Act 2018 s 13; r 85.04)? Yes. An application to have an associated trust made the proper defendant (s 8) is made by summons.
GIVEN `is my claim founded on or arising from child abuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is founded on or arises from child abuse
      `is the organisation an unincorporated NGO` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a non-government organisation that is an unincorporated association or body (such as some churches, clubs or community groups)
      `has a proper defendant been nominated or appointed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has nominated an entity as proper defendant (s 7) or a proper defendant has been appointed (s 8)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I sue the NGO in its own name` MEANS
    IF `has a proper defendant been nominated or appointed`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `is my claim founded on or arising from child abuse` AND `is the organisation an unincorporated NGO`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

-- Restated narrowly: s 7(3): "Unless the court otherwise orders, a nomination of a proper defendant may occur at any time
-- within 120 days after the commencement of the proceeding for the claim against the NGO." A period fixed by an Act, not by
-- these Rules: counted in plain days with the first day excluded, without r 3.04 or r 3.01(5) (fork F18).
@export By what day may the unincorporated organisation (NGO) I am suing for child abuse nominate a proper defendant to answer my claim (Legal Identity of Defendants (Organisational Child Abuse) Act 2018 s 7(3))? Within 120 days after my proceeding was commenced, unless the court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `the day my proceeding was commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day your proceeding against the NGO was commenced, as an ISO date
      `has the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered a different time for the nomination
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when may the NGO nominate a proper defendant` MEANS
    IF `has the court ordered otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the date` 120 `days after` `the day my proceeding was commenced`)

-- r 85.02(1): a nomination and consent under s 7 "made after the commencement of a proceeding, shall be in Form 85A and
-- include an address for service." (2): "The nomination shall be filed with the Prothonotary and served on each party to the
-- proceeding and on the entity nominated to act as a proper defendant within 7 days of the nomination being filed."
@export By what day must the NGO's nomination of a proper defendant be served on me after it is filed (r 85.02)? Within 7 days of its filing, in Form 85A with an address for service.
GIVEN `the day the nomination was filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the nomination was filed with the Prothonotary, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the nomination be served on me` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 7 `days after` `the day the nomination was filed` FALSE) FALSE

-- r 85.03: "Where a proper defendant is nominated under section 7 ..., unless the court otherwise directs, within 28 days
-- after the service of a nomination on the plaintiff -- (a) the plaintiff shall file with the Prothonotary an amended
-- originating process and statement of claim identifying the proper defendant to the claim acting on behalf of the NGO; and
-- (b) shall serve any party with the amended originating process and statement of claim sealed in accordance with Rule
-- 5.11."
-- The court having directed otherwise: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text*.
GIVEN `the day the nomination was served` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 85.03 -- the last day for the amended writ` `the day the nomination was served` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 28 `days after` `the day the nomination was served` FALSE) FALSE

@export After the NGO nominates a proper defendant, by what day must I file and serve my amended writ and statement of claim naming it (r 85.03)? Within 28 days after the nomination is served on me, unless the court directs otherwise.
GIVEN `the day the nomination was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the nomination of the proper defendant was served on you, as an ISO date
      `has the court directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed a different time
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I file my amended writ naming the proper defendant` MEANS
    IF `has the court directed otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 85.03 -- the last day for the amended writ` `the day the nomination was served on me`)

@export Did I file and serve my amended writ and statement of claim naming the proper defendant in time, or am I still in time (r 85.03)?
GIVEN `the day the nomination was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the nomination of the proper defendant was served on you, as an ISO date
      `has the court directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed a different time
      `have I filed and served it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have filed and served the amended originating process and statement of claim
      `the day I did` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day you completed it (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE `What became of my step`
`Did I file my amended writ in time` MEANS
    IF `has the court directed otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`what became of the step` `file and serve the amended originating process and statement of claim naming the proper defendant` `the day the nomination was served on me` (`r 85.03 -- the last day for the amended writ` `the day the nomination was served on me`) `have I filed and served it` `the day I did` `today`)
